    "title": "In Tennessee's MLB debut, both starters bring Knoxville connections to the mound - NewsBreak",
    "text": "KNOXVILLE, Tenn. (WATE) — The first-ever Major League Baseball game held in Tennessee will take place this weekend, and both starting pitchers who will take the mound at Bristol Motor Speedway have close ties to Knoxville . The Atlanta Braves and Cincinnati Reds will face off on Saturday at 7:20 p.m. in front of a record-breaking crowd of more than 85,000, and MLB is pulling out all the stops for the historic event. Tim McGraw and Pitbull will perform a pregame concert. The ceremonial first pitch will be thrown by Braves Hall of Famer Chipper Jones and caught by another all-time great, Reds legend Johnny Bench. Baseball fans in East Tennessee should be quite familiar with each of the game’s starting pitchers. Atlanta starter Spencer Strider helped Christian Academy of Knoxville win a state championship as a senior in 2017, earning Gatorade Tennessee Baseball Player of the Year and Tennessee Mr. Baseball honors in the process. Reds starting pitcher Chase Burns is a Tennessee native who spent two stellar seasons in Knoxville as a member of the Tennessee Volunteers. He was named 2022 National Freshman of the Year by D1Baseball.com and 2022 Freshman Pitcher of the Year by the National Collegiate Baseball Writers Association. He finished 2023 with the sixth most strikeouts among SEC pitchers despite moving to a relief role midway through the season and helped the Vols reach the College World Series. Burns entered the transfer portal and joined Wake Forest for his junior year before becoming the second overall pick in the 2024 MLB Draft. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WJHL | Tri-Cities News & Weather.",
